sections.s2_technical:
Fan-out-on-write means that when someone posts, the system immediately writes that post's id into the feed list of every follower, usually in a fast store like Redis. Reading a feed is then trivial: just read the precomputed list. This gives extremely fast reads, which matters because feeds are read far more often than posts are written. The downside is that a user with ten million followers triggers ten million writes for one post.

```java
// Fan-out-on-write: push new post into each follower's feed list
public void onNewPost(String authorId, String postId) {
    for (String follower : followers.of(authorId)) {
        feedCache.prepend(follower, postId);  // Redis LPUSH per follower
    }
}
```

Fan-out-on-read means nothing is precomputed; when a user opens their feed, the system queries the recent posts of everyone they follow and merges them. Writes are cheap, but reads are expensive and slow because they touch many accounts. This is the opposite trade-off.

The celebrity problem is the reason pure fan-out-on-write breaks. If a hugely popular account posts, fanning out to millions of feeds is wasteful, especially since many of those followers may never log in. The hybrid solution is to use fan-out-on-write for normal accounts and fan-out-on-read for celebrities: a user's feed is their precomputed list merged at read time with the latest posts from the few celebrities they follow.

```java
// Hybrid read: precomputed list + live pull from followed celebrities
public List<String> buildFeed(String userId, int limit) {
    List<String> feed = new ArrayList<>(feedCache.range(userId, 0, limit));
    for (String celeb : celebritiesFollowedBy(userId))
        feed.addAll(postStore.recent(celeb, limit));   // pull on read
    feed.sort(ranking.byScoreDesc());                   // rank, not pure time
    return feed.subList(0, Math.min(limit, feed.size()));
}
```

Ranking replaces pure chronological order with a score combining recency, engagement, and affinity, so the most relevant posts rise. Pagination uses cursors rather than offsets, because a new post arriving between page loads would shift offset-based pages and cause duplicates or gaps; a cursor based on the last seen post id or score is stable. A senior nuance is caching assembled feeds with a short time-to-live and invalidating or appending on new posts, and accepting eventual consistency, since a feed being a few seconds stale harms nobody, unlike a bank balance.

